    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm working on a loft addition for a house in oregon. gotta use a big douglas fir beam for the main span. the span is gonna be about 14 feet. i'm worried about some of the knots and potential internal check/splits in the timber i'm looking at. what should i be looking out for in terms of load bearing capacity if the wood isn't perfectly clear?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Generally, a single 14-foot Douglas fir beam is acceptable for a loft addition in Oregon, even with some knots, provided you strictly follow the span and load tables in the Oregon State Residential Code (OSCSC) and ASCE 7. You should consult a licensed structural engineer to evaluate the specific knot locations and sizes of any splits, as they can significantly reduce the beam's allowable load and overturning moment resistance compared to clear timber. The engineer may recommend avoiding certain knots near the supports, adding supplemental blocking or sistering, or specifying a stronger stiffness grade to ensure the design meets seismic and snow load requirements.",
      "source": "target",
